    "lead": "The new Q3 numbers look fine: $2.2 billion, up 5% on a year ago. Look closer, though, and the bottom of the ladder is breaking. Seed money is down 37% this year, and 30% fewer startups got their first cheque, even as Series A/B money rose 38%.",
    "body": "Think of startup funding as a ladder. Seed is the first rung, Series A/B the middle, big late rounds the top. In July–September, the middle rungs got $1.1 billion (+38% on a year ago) and four $100M+ rounds landed, up from one a year earlier. The first rung moved the other way. Early-stage money fell 18% to $164 million, and for Jan–Sep, Tracxn counts seed down 37% to about $698 million, with only 338 startups raising their first round (down 30%). Three forces are behind it, and they feed each other. First, the stock market now pays only for profit. Profitable Moneyview's IPO was booked 98×, loss-making AceVector's only 5×. So late investors want proof, Series A investors want more proof, and seed investors back fewer, safer bets. Peak XV's Rajan Anandan put it plainly this week: \"The bar to raise a Series A has gone up pretty significantly.\" Second, AI makes each startup cheaper to build, so the winners need less money. Tracxn says 2026's new unicorns raised about $101 million each before hitting $1 billion, half the $205 million earlier ones needed. Third, the seed money that is left goes into bigger cheques for fewer companies. Peak XV's Surge just raised its seed limit from $3 million to $5 million. Family offices and angels are filling some of the gap: Velumani's office led both a ₹1,750 crore EV round and a seed cheque into Prodoc AI this week. Here's the twist. The \"selective investors\" story that makes this quarter look healthy is the same thing starving the pipeline.",
